.ie \n(.g .ds Aq \(aq
.el .ds Aq '
.TH murk-agent-exec 1  "exec " 
.SH NAME
murk\-agent\-exec \- Run a command with strict agent\-safe defaults (experimental)
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmurk agent exec\fR [\fB\-\-allow\-operator\-key\fR] [\fB\-\-allow\-reserved\fR] [\fB\-\-vault\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fB\-\-only\fR> <\fICOMMAND\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Run a command with strict agent\-safe defaults (experimental)
.PP
On Unix the command keeps only PATH, HOME and TERM from the inherited environment. On Windows it keeps PATH and a fixed set of system variables. murk strips MURK_KEY, requires `\-\-only` and masks the injected values in the output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-only\fR \fI<ONLY>\fR
Inject only these secrets (repeatable, required)
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-operator\-key\fR
Decrypt with a key that is not an agent grant: your own stored key from ~/.config/murk/keys, or a full recipient\*(Aqs MURK_KEY or MURK_KEY_FILE. Without this, agent exec needs an agent grant key
.TP
\fB\-\-allow\-reserved\fR
Allow secrets named like loader, shell or tool variables (PATH, LD_*, GIT_*, MURK_*, ...)
.TP
\fB\-\-vault\fR \fI<VAULT>\fR [default: .murk]
Vault filename
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fICOMMAND\fR>
Command and arguments to run

Put murk options such as `\-\-only` before the command. murk passes everything after the command name to the command, so `murk agent exec echo hi \-\-only FOO` fails with a missing `\-\-only`.
